---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import FrostMachineBackground from "../components/react/FrostMachineBackground";
import Navbar from "../components/react/Navbar";
import SiteFooter from "../components/react/SiteFooter";
import Breadcrumbs from "../components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
import { history, version } from "../data/product";

/*
 * Privacy.
 *
 * Every claim here was read out of the source rather than adapted from a
 * template, because a privacy page that overstates is worse than none:
 *
 * - "no network call" is bin/gcoolers containing no urllib, requests, socket or
 *   subprocess-curl call. The only URLs in it are two string constants (the
 *   site and the repo) and Apple's plist DTD identifier, which is an XML
 *   namespace, not a fetch.
 * - The file list and the retention numbers are SUPPORT/*.json, LOG_PATH and
 *   history_append() — 3600s cutoff, 360 samples, 40 events.
 * - The site really does ship no analytics: there is no gtag, no tag manager,
 *   no pixel, no beacon, and no fetch anywhere in src/.
 * - Google Fonts is disclosed because it is a real third-party request that
 *   carries the visitor's IP, and pretending otherwise would be the exact
 *   failure this page exists to avoid.
 */
const appFiles = [
  { path: "config.json", what: "Your settings: profile, meeting mode, schedule, notifications." },
  { path: "state.json", what: "The current reading — peak temperature, zone, fan percentage, and why." },
  {
    path: "history.json",
    what: `A rolling ${history.windowMinutes} minutes of samples for the menu bar chart. At most ${history.maxSamples} samples and ${history.maxEvents} MAX events; anything older than ${history.windowMinutes} minutes is dropped on the next write.`,
  },
  {
    path: "learn.json",
    what: "The adaptive bias per workload shape, plus the names of applications you have associated with meeting mode or a profile. Application names are stored here, on your Mac, and are never sent anywhere.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy"
  description="What Gcoolers stores and where. The governor makes no network calls and keeps everything in local files; the website has no analytics and no cookies, and loads one third-party resource."
  path="/privacy"
  crumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Privacy" }]}
>
  <FrostMachineBackground client:load />
  <Navbar client:load path="/privacy" />

  <main id="main">
    <section class="page wrap">
      <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Privacy" }]} />

      <div class="page-head">
        <p class="mono">Privacy · what is stored, and where</p>
        <h1>Nothing leaves your Mac.</h1>
        <span class="page-rule" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <p>
          This page describes what the software and this website actually do. Everything below was
          checked against the source for v{version} rather than written from a template — if you find
          a difference between this page and the code, the code is the thing to trust, and please{" "}
          <a href={site.issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">open an issue</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel prose-panel">
        <h2>The governor</h2>
        <p>
          Gcoolers has no account, no sign-in, and no telemetry. There is no analytics code and no
          network call anywhere in the governor — it reads your Mac's sensors, writes to local files,
          and sets fan targets. It does not phone home, check for updates, or report crashes.
        </p>

        <p>
          Everything it keeps lives in <code>~/Library/Application&nbsp;Support/Gcoolers</code>:
        </p>

        <dl class="privacy-files">
          {
            appFiles.map((f) => (
              <div class="privacy-file">
                <dt><code>{f.path}</code></dt>
                <dd>{f.what}</dd>
              </div>
            ))
          }
        </dl>

        <p>
          It also appends to <code>~/Library/Logs/gcoolers.log</code>. That log is only ever appended
          to — it is not rotated or trimmed, so it grows until you delete it, and deleting it is safe
          at any time.
        </p>

        <p>
          <code class="code-inline">gcoolers export</code> writes a CSV and an HTML file of the last
          hour into <code>~/Downloads</code>, and only when you run it. Those files are yours; nothing
          uploads them.
        </p>

        <p>
          To remove all of it: delete <code>~/Library/Application&nbsp;Support/Gcoolers</code> and
          <code>~/Library/Logs/gcoolers.log</code>. The full removal steps, including the privileged
          helper, are in the <a href="/#faq">FAQ</a>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel prose-panel">
        <h2>This website</h2>
        <p>
          These pages are static files served by GitHub Pages. There is no analytics, no tag manager,
          no advertising pixel, and no cookie set by this site.
        </p>

        <p>
          The site stores exactly one thing in your browser: a
          <code>sessionStorage</code> entry called <code>gcoolers:booted</code>. It records that you
          have already seen the opening animation, so a second page view in the same tab gets a short
          version instead of the full one. Your browser discards it when you close the tab, and it
          contains no identifier.
        </p>

        <h3>Third parties</h3>
        <ul class="privacy-third">
          <li>
            <b>Google Fonts.</b> The page loads three typefaces from
            <code>fonts.googleapis.com</code> and <code>fonts.gstatic.com</code>. Google receives your
            IP address and browser user-agent as part of serving those files. This is the only
            third-party request the site makes on load.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>GitHub Pages.</b> GitHub hosts and serves these files, so GitHub receives the usual web
            server request information, including your IP address, under its own privacy statement.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Links you choose to follow.</b> GitHub, Buy&nbsp;Me&nbsp;a&nbsp;Coffee and Cash&nbsp;App
            are ordinary links on the <a href="/support">support page</a>. Nothing loads from them
            unless you click, and any payment happens on their site under their terms, not here.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="panel prose-panel">
        <h2>Field reports</h2>
        <p>
          If you submit a review on the <a href="/reviews">field reports</a> page, that form is the one
          place this site sends anything anywhere. It posts to a small API run on Cloudflare Workers with a
          Cloudflare D1 database — a processor acting for this project, not a third party you have a
          relationship with.
        </p>

        <h3>What a submission stores</h3>
        <ul class="privacy-third">
          <li>
            <b>What you wrote.</b> Display name, rating, title and report, plus any optional context you
            chose to add: Mac model, macOS version, Gcoolers version, profile, use case, and a link. Only
            the moderated copy is stored — if the filter masked a word, the masked version is what exists,
            and the original is discarded rather than kept in a private column.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Whether you asked to be published.</b> The consent tick is stored with the report and
            honoured.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>A salted hash of your IP address, and your browser's user-agent string.</b> The address
            itself is never written down: it is combined with a secret salt and hashed, and only the hash
            is kept. It exists to make rate limiting possible and to let obvious abuse be traced between
            submissions. It cannot be turned back into your address.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Moderation state.</b> The review's status, and category codes such as
            <code>body:spam_keyword</code> if anything was flagged. Codes only — never a copy of the text
            that triggered them.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>What it never stores</h3>
        <p>
          The form does not ask for, and the server rejects, a postal address, phone number, legal name,
          password, device serial, or any hardware identifier. There is no account and no tracking
          identifier: two reports from the same person are not linked to each other.
        </p>

        <h3>Spam prevention</h3>
        <p>
          Submissions are rate limited against the hashed address described above, and the form carries a
          hidden field that only automated tools fill in. There is no CAPTCHA, no fingerprinting, and no
          third-party bot service — nothing about your submission is sent to anyone else to be scored.
        </p>

        <h3>Removing a report</h3>
        <p>
          Email <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> from any address, or
          <a href={site.issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"> open an issue</a>, and say which
          report is yours. It will be taken down. You do not have to explain why, and a removed report stops
          being public immediately.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="panel prose-panel">
        <h2>Getting in touch</h2>
        <p>
          There is no contact form and no mailing list. If you email
          <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>, your message and address sit in that
          mailbox so it can be answered, and they are not added to any list or shared. If you would
          rather not use email, <a href={site.issues} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            a GitHub issue</a
          > is public and works just as well.
        </p>
        <p class="privacy-foot mono">
          Checked against Gcoolers v{version} · source: <a
            href={site.github}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">{site.github.replace("https://", "")}</a
          >
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
